body_markdown:
To generate a certificate PDF in Node.js, render a React layout with Unlayer Elements’ Document wrapper, then pass that HTML to Puppeteer. Use the same completion record for the certificate and its notification email so the learner’s name, course and certificate reference stay consistent.


We’ll start with one certificate written to disk, without contacting an email server. Then we’ll add an Elements email and an explicit SMTP send step with the PDF attached. This is code-first content generation, not an embedded visual editor or a learning-management system.


## What is the smallest working certificate generator?


Use a Node.js project with a package.json and Node 22.12 or later, as required by the current


[Puppeteer release](https://pptr.dev/guides/system-requirements) .


[Elements requires React and React DOM 18 or later](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/installation) . Install the content, browser and email packages, plus tsx for running the TypeScript example:


```text
npm     install   @unlayer/react-elements react react-dom puppeteer nodemailer
npm     install   -D tsx
```


Puppeteer normally downloads its compatible browser during installation. If your package manager blocks dependency install scripts, follow its


[browser installation instructions](https://pptr.dev/guides/installation) :


```text
npx puppeteer browsers install
```


Keep your lockfile. The browser also needs the operating-system libraries listed in Puppeteer’s requirements. Don’t treat installing the JavaScript package alone as proof that a production container can launch Chrome.


Create certificate.tsx. The explicit React import also supports projects using the classic JSX transform. This first version uses one completion record and a direct Document tree:


```text
import     React     from     'react'  ;
import     {   mkdir  ,   writeFile   }     from     'node:fs/promises'  ;
import     puppeteer     from     'puppeteer'  ;
import     nodemailer     from     'nodemailer'  ;
import     {
Document  ,     Email  ,     Row  ,     Column  ,     Heading  ,     Paragraph  ,   renderToHtmlParts  ,
}     from     '@unlayer/react-elements'  ;


const   completion   =     {
id  :     'FN-2026-1048'  ,
learner  :     'Morgan Rivera'  ,
course  :     'Building Reliable Web Applications'  ,
completedOn  :     '2 November 2026'  ,
}  ;
const   fontFamily   =     {
label  :     'Arial'  ,     value  :     'Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'  ,
}  ;
const   certificate   =     (
<  Document   backgroundColor  =  "#ffffff"   contentWidth  =  "10in"
fontFamily  =  {  fontFamily  }  >
<  Row   padding  =  "44px 48px"   backgroundColor  =  "#f4f7f5"  >  <  Column  >
<  Paragraph   textAlign  =  "center"   color  =  "#24534a"   fontSize  =  "16px"  >
FIELDNOTE     ACADEMY
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  Heading   headingType  =  "h1"   textAlign  =  "center"
color  =  "#173e35"   fontSize  =  "38px"  >
Certificate     of     Completion
<  /  Heading  >
<  Paragraph   textAlign  =  "center"   fontSize  =  "18px"  >
This   certificate is awarded to
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  Heading   headingType  =  "h2"   textAlign  =  "center"
color  =  "#173e35"   fontSize  =  "32px"  >  {  completion  .  learner  }  <  /  Heading  >
<  Paragraph   textAlign  =  "center"   fontSize  =  "18px"  >  for   completing  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Heading   headingType  =  "h2"   textAlign  =  "center"
color  =  "#173e35"   fontSize  =  "26px"  >  {  completion  .  course  }  <  /  Heading  >
<  Paragraph   textAlign  =  "center"   fontSize  =  "16px"  >
Completed   on   {  completion  .  completedOn  }
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph   textAlign  =  "center"   color  =  "#526b63"   fontSize  =  "14px"  >
Certificate   reference  :     {  completion  .  id  }
<  /  Paragraph  >
Issued   by   Fieldnote     Academy
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  /  Column  >  <  /  Row  >
<  /  Document  >
)  ;
function     fullHtml  (  tree  :     React  .  ReactElement  )     {
const     {   head  ,   body   }     =     renderToHtmlParts  (  tree  )  ;
return     `  <!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head>  ${  head  }  </head>  ${  body  }  </html>  `  ;
}


async     function     main  (  )     {
const   html   =     fullHtml  (  certificate  )  ;
await     mkdir  (  'out'  ,     {     recursive  :     true     }  )  ;
await     writeFile  (  'out/certificate.html'  ,   html  ,     'utf8'  )  ;
const   browser   =     await   puppeteer  .  launch  (  )  ;
let     pdf  :     Uint8Array  ;
try     {
const   page   =     await   browser  .  newPage  (  )  ;
await   page  .  setContent  (  html  )  ;
pdf   =     await   page  .  pdf  (  {
format  :     'letter'  ,     landscape  :     true  ,     printBackground  :     true  ,
margin  :     {     top  :     '0.5in'  ,     right  :     '0.5in'  ,     bottom  :     '0.5in'  ,     left  :     '0.5in'     }  ,
preferCSSPageSize  :     false  ,     waitForFonts  :     true  ,
}  )  ;
}     finally     {
await   browser  .  close  (  )  ;
}
await     writeFile  (  'out/certificate.pdf'  ,   pdf  )  ;
console  .  log  (  'Certificate files written to out/'  )  ;
}
main  (  )  .  catch  (  error     =>     {
console  .  error  (  error  )  ;
process  .  exitCode     =     1  ;
}  )  ;
```


Run the file with


[tsx](https://tsx.hirok.io/getting-started) :


```text
npx tsx certificate.tsx
```


Open out/certificate.html to inspect the content, then open out/certificate.pdf in a PDF viewer. Review the learner, course, completion date, margins and page count before sending anything. The PDF is the artifact that matters for printing; a browser layout preview is useful, but it cannot establish the final pagination.


*The Document layout keeps the learner, course, completion date and certificate reference together.*


## Which part makes the PDF?


Elements produces the content. Its


[Document wrapper](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/wrappers) targets print-oriented HTML and is explicitly intended for certificates, invoices and reports. It does not create PDF bytes itself. Puppeteer loads that HTML and


[prints it to a PDF](https://pptr.dev/api/puppeteer.page.pdf) .


The example uses the documented


[renderToHtmlParts composition](https://docs.unlayer.com/elements/react/rendering-api) : styles go into head, and the returned body follows it. Keep the wrapper at the renderer boundary, with Row, Column and content inside. There’s no browser-side React hydration step in this workflow.


Puppeteer returns a Uint8Array, which


[Node’s writeFile accepts](https://nodejs.org/api/fs.html) . The try/finally closes the browser even when content loading or PDF generation fails. A generation failure stops the function before the email step, rather than submitting a message that claims to contain an unavailable certificate.


## How do you control certificate size and long names?


This layout targets landscape US Letter. The 10-inch content width leaves room for the configured half-inch side margins.


[PDFOptions](https://pptr.dev/api/puppeteer.pdfoptions) documents format, landscape, margins and printBackground. Avoid mixing format with competing width and height values: format takes priority. Here preferCSSPageSize is false, so an inherited CSS page size does not override the selected paper format.


Don’t add pageRanges: '1' to hide overflow. That can omit certificate content instead of fixing it. Review the actual PDF with long learner names and course titles, and adjust the layout deliberately. A single short-name preview is not enough to establish a one-page contract for every completion record.


The first version uses system fonts and no external images. Before adding a logo or custom font, make its availability predictable in the rendering environment and review the resulting PDF. Waiting for fonts does not mean the intended font exists. Keep essential information as text rather than baking the whole certificate into an image.


## How do you email the certificate as an attachment?


Add the following function above main(). It renders a separate Email layout from the same record, authors a plain-text alternative and attaches the PDF bytes using


[Nodemailer’s Buffer attachment option](https://nodemailer.com/message/attachments) .


```text
function     required  (  key  :   string  )     {
const   value   =   process  .  env  [  key  ]  ;
if     (  !  value  )     throw     new     Error  (  `  Missing   ${  key  }  `  )  ;
return   value  ;
}
async     function     sendCertificate  (  pdf  :     Uint8Array  )     {
const   tree   =     (
<  Email   backgroundColor  =  "#edf1f0"   contentWidth  =  "600px"
previewText  =  "Your course certificate is attached."   fontFamily  =  {  fontFamily  }  >
<  Row   padding  =  "28px 32px"   backgroundColor  =  "#173e35"  >  <  Column  >
<  Paragraph   color  =  "#c6e2d9"   fontSize  =  "14px"  >  FIELDNOTE     ACADEMY  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Heading   headingType  =  "h1"   color  =  "#ffffff"   fontSize  =  "28px"  >
Your   certificate is ready
<  /  Heading  >
<  /  Column  >  <  /  Row  >
<  Row   padding  =  "24px 32px"   backgroundColor  =  "#ffffff"  >  <  Column  >
<  Paragraph  >  Hi     {  completion  .  learner  }  ,  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph  >
Congratulations   on completing   {  completion  .  course  }   on   {  completion  .  completedOn  }  .
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph  >  Your   certificate is attached   as   a   PDF  .  <  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph   color  =  "#526b63"   fontSize  =  "14px"  >
Certificate   reference  :     {  completion  .  id  }
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  Paragraph   color  =  "#526b63"   fontSize  =  "14px"  >
Keep   it   for   your records  .     If   your name needs a correction  ,   reply to   this   email  .
<  /  Paragraph  >
<  /  Column  >  <  /  Row  >
<  /  Email  >
)  ;
const   html   =     fullHtml  (  tree  )  ;
const   text   =     [
`  Hi   ${  completion  .  learner  }  ,  `  ,
`  Congratulations on completing   ${  completion  .  course  }   on   ${  completion  .  completedOn  }  .  `  ,
'Your certificate is attached as a PDF.'  ,
`  Certificate reference:   ${  completion  .  id  }  `  ,
'Keep it for your records. If your name needs a correction, reply to this email.'  ,
]  .  join  (  '\n\n'  )  ;
await     writeFile  (  'out/notification.html'  ,   html  ,     'utf8'  )  ;
const   transporter   =   nodemailer  .  createTransport  (  {
host  :     required  (  'SMTP_HOST'  )  ,     port  :     587  ,     secure  :     false  ,     requireTLS  :     true  ,
auth  :     {     user  :     required  (  'SMTP_USER'  )  ,     pass  :     required  (  'SMTP_PASS'  )     }  ,
disableFileAccess  :     true  ,     disableUrlAccess  :     true  ,
}  )  ;
const   info   =     await   transporter  .  sendMail  (  {
from  :     required  (  'MAIL_FROM'  )  ,     to  :     required  (  'MAIL_TO'  )  ,
subject  :     `  Your certificate:   ${  completion  .  course  }  `  ,   html  ,   text  ,
attachments  :     [  {
filename  :     'course-certificate.pdf'  ,     content  :     Buffer  .  from  (  pdf  )  ,
contentType  :     'application/pdf'  ,
}  ]  ,
}  )  ;
if     (  info  .  rejected  .  length     ||     !  info  .  accepted  .  length  )     {
throw     new     Error  (  'Recipient acceptance needs review'  )  ;
}
console  .  log  (  'SMTP accepted the message:'  ,   info  .  messageId  )  ;
}
```


Add this line at the end of main(), after the PDF write:


```text
if (process.argv.includes('--send')) await sendCertificate(pdf);
```


Set SMTP_HOST, SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASS from your provider, MAIL_FROM to an authorized sender and MAIL_TO to one mailbox you control. Keep those values in server environment variables, not committed source. Use a monitored sender because the message invites replies.


The


[SMTP configuration](https://nodemailer.com/smtp) requires port 587 with STARTTLS. If your provider requires port 465, use its documented secure: true settings instead. Don’t disable certificate validation to bypass a connection failure. With the fields reviewed and credentials configured, opt into submission:


```text
npx tsx certificate.tsx --send
```


Running it again attempts another email. SMTP acceptance is not proof of inbox delivery. The saved notification.html lets you inspect the prepared content, but it is not a substitute for reviewing the received attachment and message in your supported clients.


*The notification repeats the same completion details and tells the learner where to find the PDF.*


## What should change before this becomes an automated job?


Replace the hard-coded record with an authorized completion loaded by your application. The application must decide whether the learner earned the certificate, which tenant issued it and which recipient may receive it. Don’t expose an endpoint that accepts arbitrary names, completion claims, HTML or recipient addresses and turns them into certificates.


Persist a stable certificate reference and an immutable issuance revision. A correction should be an explicit reissue, not an unnoticed change to a file someone already received. The printed reference is useful for support; it is not a cryptographic signature or proof that a certificate is authentic.


Keep names and course titles as React text children. Don’t replace them with untrusted HTML. If you later allow remote images, isolate the browser worker and restrict its network access. Nodemailer’s disableUrlAccess restricts Nodemailer, not requests made by Puppeteer while rendering a page.


Before adding retries, persist the issuance record, PDF artifact and notification state. Coordinate workers using an application-level notification key. A timeout after SMTP submission can leave the outcome uncertain, so reconcile it before blindly resending. Neither Elements nor Nodemailer supplies exactly-once delivery.


For broader product context, our


[industry use-case guide](https://unlayer.com/blog/unlayer-use-cases-benefits) discusses education workflows. If your next notification needs an add-to-calendar file instead of a PDF, the


[Node.js calendar-email tutorial](https://unlayer.com/blog/nodejs-email-calendar-invite-react) covers that separate attachment format.


## Frequently asked questions


### Can Unlayer Elements generate PDF bytes directly?


No. Document renders print-oriented HTML. This implementation uses Puppeteer for the PDF and Nodemailer for submission. Course completion, recipient authorization and issuance history stay in your application.


### Can customers edit this certificate in a visual builder?


Not in this first version. It is a code-authored layout. Adding customer editing is a separate builder integration with its own design persistence and access controls. Visual edits do not reconstruct your React source.


### Does the certificate prove that someone completed the course?


The file communicates your application’s completion decision. It doesn’t verify that decision or implement credential verification, revocation or signed documents. Those require a separate issuance and verification design.


